rom 1; isolated vertices, when there are any, are layer 1 alone."}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/AllCliques":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/AllCliques","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every clique of an undirected graph’s simple graph whose size is in a range, once each, lazily,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each in "},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" order (by vertex index). The order is by the cliques’ first vertex in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"coreNumbers().degeneracyOrdering","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", then depth first, each clique followed by its extensions by"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"later vertices in ascending index. The sequence holds the graph, so iterating again gives the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d) bitset words per clique for degeneracy d."}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/allcliques","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"AllCliques"}],"title":"AllCliques","type":"topic","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"AllCliques"}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/ClusteringCoefficients":{"title":"ClusteringCoefficients","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/ClusteringCoefficients","abstract":[{"text":"Triangles and clustering for every vertex of an undirected graph’s simple graph, from one","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"pass.","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/clusteringcoefficients","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ClusteringCoefficients"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ClusteringCoefficients"}],"type":"topic","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/CoreNumbers":{"title":"CoreNumbers","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/corenumbers","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/CoreNumbers","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"CoreNumbers","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The core decomposition of an undirected graph (Batagelj–Zaversnik), over its simple graph. A"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertex’s core number is the largest k such that it lies in a subgraph where every vertex has at"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"least k neighbors."}],"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"CoreNumbers","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/GraphProtocols":{"title":"GraphProtocols","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/GraphProtocols","abstract":[],"url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/graphprotocols","role":"collection","kind":"symbol","type":"topic"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/TrussNumbers":{"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"TrussNumbers"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","title":"TrussNumbers","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/trussnumbers","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/TrussNumbers","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"TrussNumbers"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The truss decomposition of an undirected graph (Cohen; Wang–Cheng), over its simple graph. An"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"edge’s truss number is the largest k such that it lies in a subgraph where every edge is in at"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"least k − 2 triangles: 2 for an edge in no triangle, k for an edge of a k-clique. Parallel"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"edges share their simple edge’s number, and a self-loop has none.","type":"text"}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/MaximalCliques":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/MaximalCliques","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","title":"MaximalCliques","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/maximalcliques","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every maximal clique of an undirected graph’s simple graph once, lazily, each in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"order (by vertex index): an isolated vertex, or one whose only edges are loops, is a clique of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one. The order is Eppstein–Löffler–Strash’s: by the cliques’ first vertex in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"coreNumbers().degeneracyOrdering"},{"type":"text","text":", then as Bron–Kerbosch with Tomita’s pivot (ties to the least"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"index) and branches in ascending index meets them. The sequence holds the graph, so iterating"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"again gives the same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d·n·3^(d\/3)) in all for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"degeneracy d."}],"type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}]}}}